s impossible** due to W4 being monopolized.\n\nAlternative: **Full Flush** (24 points) \u2014 all Characters. Possible if we keep W-tiles and ditch honors and other suits.\n\nCurrent hand:  \nW5, W7, W8, W9, W9  \nT1, T5, T7 (and meld [T2,T3,T4])  \nB2, B7  \nF4\n\nWe must discard non-Character tiles eventually. Best chance is to go for **Full Flush**, which requires all tiles to be same suit (here, W). That\u2019s 24 points \u2014 solid base.\n\nTo pursue Full Flush:\n- Need to collect more W6, W1-W3, W4 (but W4 is gone), so focus on W6, W7, W8, W9, W5.\nBut W4 is out, so 4-5-6 is blocked.\n\nWait \u2014 we have W5, W7, W8, W9. Missing W6 to make W5-6-7 and W7-8-9.\n\nIf we can draw W6, then we can form:\n- W5-6-7 (need W6 + W7)\n- W7-8-9\nBut we\u2019d need another W6 and W7 \u2014 tight.\n\nAlternatively, aim for **Upper Tiles** (24 points): only 7,8,9 suited tiles. We have:\n- W7, W8, W9\u00d72 \u2192 good start\n- T7 \u2192 could keep\n- But T1, T5, B2, B7 are low numbers \u2192 bad fit\n\nSo **Upper Tiles** possible only if we ditch T1, T5, B2, B7.\n\nBut B7 and T7 could help with that.\n\nStill, better to go for **Full Flush** since we already have four W-tiles.\n\nNow evaluate discards:\n\n- **Play T1**: Safe, not useful, terminal. Good discard.\n- **Play T5**: Middle bamboo, not helpful. Also safe.\n- **Play B2**: Useless, not in any sequence.\n- **Play B7**: Next player just discarded B7 \u2192 very safe to discard.\n- **Play F4**: Seat wind, but unlikely to get more. Risk holding it.\n- Among W-tiles: W5 is most isolated. W7, W8, W9 form high sequence.\n\nBest move: **Discard a non-Character tile that doesn\u2019t help opponents**.\n\nB7 was just discarded by next player \u2192 safe.\n\nT1, T5 also safe.\n\nBut **Play B7** is safest because it was just discarded \u2014 unlikely anyone is waiting on it.\n\nHowever, we want to prioritize **suit uniformity**. So better to discard **T1** or **T5** to reduce Bamboo presence.\n\nT1 is terminal, could be wanted for Outside Hand, but less common.\n\nBut **Play T1** removes a terminal early, helps streamline toward Full Flush.\n\nAlternatively, **Play F4** \u2014 honor tile, and we\u2019re not building All Honors or Three Dragons.\n\nSince we have no dragon pungs, and F4 is not prevalent wind (West = F3), no value now.\n\nThus, **Play F4** is acceptable.\n\nBut better to remove **non-Character suited tiles first**, to signal hand type and reduce clutter.\n\nSo: **Play T1** or **Play T5**\n\nBetween them, T1 is more dangerous (terminal), but we don\u2019t need it.\n\nAlso, previous player discarded J1 twice, and F2 \u2014 seems to be dumping honors. Maybe not collecting.\n\nNo evidence of someone building All Honors.\n\nSo **Play F4** is fine.\n\nBut optimal: **reduce non-Character tiles first**.\n\nFinal consideration: **Are we close to any high-scoring hand?**\n\nNot yet. Best path: **Full Flush (24)** + possibly **All Chows (2)** + **No Honors (1)** if we drop F4.\n\nBut we have a pung of W9 possible if we draw another \u2014 but only two left (unshown: W9:4, we have 2 \u2192 2 left).\n\nBut hard to rely.\n\nBetter to simplify.\n\nTop candidate discards:  \n- **Play T1**: removes terminal, starts clean-up  \n- **Play B7**: safe (just discarded), removes extra suit  \n- **Play F4**: removes honor, reduces chance of Chicken Hand\n\nBut **Play T1** is best because:\n- It\u2019s a terminal in a weak suit\n- Doesn\u2019t feed any obvious chow\n- Helps us move toward Full Flush by reducing reliance on T/B\n\nAlso, we already have a chow in T2-T3-T4, so keeping T1 doesn\u2019t help (no T2 to pair), whereas T5 could help 4-5-6 if we had T4/T6, but we don\u2019t.\n\nWait \u2014 we have T2-T3-T4 melded, so T1 could form 1-2-3? But we already used T2-T3-T4 \u2014 cannot reuse T2/T3.\n\nSo T1 is useless.\n\nSimilarly, T5 and T7 are loose.\n\nBut T5 and T7 could be used in future chows if we stay in mixed suit, but we should commit to **Characters**.\n\nTherefore, best to **discard T1** to begin shedding Bamboo.\n\nAlso, **W5** is somewhat weak \u2014 could be sacrificed later \u2014 but for now, keep as potential 5-6-7.\n\nBut among valid actions, **Play T1** is the cleanest discard.\n\nReason: T1 is an isolated terminal tile in a different suit, not contributing to any potential high-scoring pattern in the current context. Keeping it hinders the development of a Full Flush, which is our most viable high-point path (requiring all tiles in one suit). Discarding it begins streamlining the hand.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
